               <t:note>
                  <t:ab type="history">Waǧ is a historical region in <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC5597Sawa">Šawā</t:placeName> 
                south of the river <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC4892Mugar">Mugär</t:placeName>
                     which flows into the <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC1022Abbay">ʾAbbāy</t:placeName> west of <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/INS0341DL">Dabra Libānos</t:placeName> 
                of <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC5597Sawa">Šawā</t:placeName>.
                     It was one of the seven districts of <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC5597Sawa">Šawā</t:placeName> 
                which contributed to troops to <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS10303Yekunno">Yǝkunno ʾAmlāk</t:persName>
                     for his campaign against the last Zāgʷe king. It was also the native region
                     of <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS6734MarhaKr">Yǝmrǝḥanna Krǝstos</t:persName> the 15th century 9th 
                  <t:roleName type="title">ʾǝč̣č̣age</t:roleName> of 
                <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/INS0341DL">Dabra Libānos</t:placeName>. The history of 
                Waǧ is closely connected to  its neighbour on the east 
                <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC3061Fataga">Faṭagār</t:placeName>. <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS1854Amdase">ʿAmda Ṣǝyon I</t:persName> 
                is reported to have defeated the Zebdār of Waǧ  
                  and appointed a governor with the title Masfǝn. During the time
                  of <t:roleName type="title">ʾaṣe</t:roleName>
                     <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS10626ZaraY">Zarʾa Yāʿqob</t:persName> the governor bore the title hǝgāno,
                a possible indication that the district was Islamic by that time. During the time 
                of <t:roleName type="title">ʾaṣe</t:roleName>  
                     <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS2334BaedaM">Baʾǝda Māryām I</t:persName> it served as an
                important strategic region to conduct military campaigns against
                <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC1095Adal">ʾAdāl</t:placeName>. 
                  Following the death of <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS1522Ahmadb">ʾAḥmad</t:persName>, 
                Waǧ was also a stronghold of King <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS4428Galawdew">Galāwdewos</t:persName> where he built his royal seat 
                  in<t:date>1550</t:date>. In <t:date>1577</t:date>, <t:roleName type="title">ʾaṣe</t:roleName> 
                     <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS8550sardaDe">Śarḍa Dǝngǝl</t:persName>, on his way to attack 
                     sultān of <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC1095Adal">ʾAdāl</t:placeName>, passed through Waǧ.</t:ab>
               </t:note>               
